-# a product this small (m n k, the multiply-adds) is computed by gemmDirect: packing would cost more than it saves
-smallProduct I64 = 64 * 64 * 64
+# whether a product of m x k by k x n is computed by gemmDirect rather than packed: one so small that the packed path's
+# own costs outweigh what it saves - at most smallProduct multiply-adds - or a result of fewer than twelve rows, each at
+# least four vectors long, against a right operand that stays in the cache (k n at most 65536 elements): the direct
+# loop then runs along the rows at full width, where a tile would be mostly padding. Measured against the packed path
+# on AVX-512, AVX2 and SSE (bench/README.md). The bound this replaced, 64^3 multiply-adds of any shape, made a product
+# as small as attention's 64 x 32 x 64 two to three times slower than packing it on AVX-512, and only the shapes above
+# ever came out ahead
+smallProduct I64 = 1024
+
+fn computedDirectly(m I64, n I64, k I64, z <T>) Bool {
+    return m * n * k <= smallProduct or (m < 12 and n >= 4 * vectorLanes(z) and k * n <= 65536)
+}
 
 # a small product, in the element type and without packing: each row of C updated from rows of B (the i-k-j loop,
